# [[-ship]]; {{ng|forms abstract nouns from adjectives and nouns}}
#* {{quote-text|ang|year=c. 1000|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=fEjXOCbwu4sC&lpg=PA52&vq=w%C3%A6tersciepes&pg=PA52#v=snippet&q=w%C3%A6tersciepes&f=false|title=The Letter of Alexander the Great to Aristotle|passage=''Ða toforan monan upgonge þa cwomon þær Scorpiones þæt wyrmcyn swa hie ær gewunelice wæron toweard þæs wæter'''sciepes'''.''
|translation= Then before moonrise those vermin called scorpions came, as they were accustomed, toward the water source.}}

====Usage notes====
* Though an expected outcome of {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*-skapiz}} in the Early West Saxon dialect, the form {{m|ang|-sċiepe}} itself is a {{glossary|hapax legomenon}}, attested only once within the corpus (seen in the quotation above). The form {{m|ang|-sċipe}} is what is primarily found instead, reflecting low stress monophthongization and raising in all dialects prior to the written period. Compare also {{m|ang|hīew}} and {{m|ang|nīewe}} for similar changes.

